20e0ae0f (Dengnifer 2026-08-30 13:55:01 +0800 1214) \section{Typed conditionally linear distributions}
20e0ae0f (Dengnifer 2026-08-30 13:55:01 +0800 1215) % Paper section label sec:types kept for cross-reference fidelity. Only the mathematical
20e0ae0f (Dengnifer 2026-08-30 13:55:01 +0800 1216) % typed material (typed CL functions and distributions, graph distributions) is stated
20e0ae0f (Dengnifer 2026-08-30 13:55:01 +0800 1217) % here; the typed sampler and verifier machinery of the source is outside the scope of
20e0ae0f (Dengnifer 2026-08-30 13:55:01 +0800 1218) % the mathematical development.
20e0ae0f (Dengnifer 2026-08-30 13:55:01 +0800 1219) \label{sec:types}
20e0ae0f (Dengnifer 2026-08-30 13:55:01 +0800 1220) 
20e0ae0f (Dengnifer 2026-08-30 13:55:01 +0800 1221) The question distribution of the Pauli basis test is \emph{typed}: each question is a pair consisting of a type, drawn together with the other player's type from the edges of a fixed \emph{type graph}, and a question content, drawn from a CL distribution determined by the pair of types.
20e0ae0f (Dengnifer 2026-08-30 13:55:01 +0800 1222) 
20e0ae0f (Dengnifer 2026-08-30 13:55:01 +0800 1223) \begin{definition}[Typed conditionally linear functions]\label{def:typed-cl-functions}
b075afd3 (Dengnifer 2026-09-02 21:23:47 +0800 1224) 	\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.IsTypedCondLinearFamily}
b075afd3 (Dengnifer 2026-09-02 21:23:47 +0800 1225) 	\leanok
20e0ae0f (Dengnifer 2026-08-30 13:55:01 +0800 1226) 	\uses{def:cl-func}
20e0ae0f (Dengnifer 2026-08-30 13:55:01 +0800 1227) 	Let $\mathcal T$ be a finite set (the \emph{type set}) and let $V$ be $\F^n$ for some integer $n \ge 0$. A \emph{$\mathcal T$-typed family of $\ell$-level conditionally linear functions} (implicitly, \emph{on $V$}) is a collection $\{L_t\}_{t \in \mathcal T}$ such that, for each $t \in \mathcal T$, $L_t$ is an $\ell$-level conditionally linear function on $V$.
20e0ae0f (Dengnifer 2026-08-30 13:55:01 +0800 1228) \end{definition}
20e0ae0f (Dengnifer 2026-08-30 13:55:01 +0800 1229) 
99d235cf (Dengnifer 2026-08-30 15:57:00 +0800 1230) % Nonemptiness of the edge set is stated explicitly here: with E empty, m = k = 0 and the
99d235cf (Dengnifer 2026-08-30 15:57:00 +0800 1231) % normalization 1/(2m-k) below is undefined. The source
99d235cf (Dengnifer 2026-08-30 15:57:00 +0800 1232) % (references/qpbt-paper/07_types.tex:65-82) leaves this boundary hypothesis implicit.
20e0ae0f (Dengnifer 2026-08-30 13:55:01 +0800 1233) \begin{definition}[Graph distribution]\label{def:graph-distribution}
4c7e7295 (Dengnifer 2026-09-04 17:19:42 +0800 1234) 	\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.graphDistribution}
b288e531 (Dengnifer 2026-09-04 16:37:45 +0800 1235) 	\leanok
99d235cf (Dengnifer 2026-08-30 15:57:00 +0800 1236) 	Let $G = (U, E)$ be an undirected graph with vertex set $U$ and nonempty edge set $E$. Edges in $E$ are written as multisets $\{u, v\}$ of two vertices; the case $u = v$ represents a self-loop. Suppose there are $m \ge 1$ edges, $k$ of which are self-loops. Then the \emph{graph distribution $\mu_G$ of $G$} is the distribution over $U \times U$ such that for every $(u,v) \in U \times U$,
20e0ae0f (Dengnifer 2026-08-30 13:55:01 +0800 1237) 	\[
20e0ae0f (Dengnifer 2026-08-30 13:55:01 +0800 1238) 		\mu_G(u, v) =
20e0ae0f (Dengnifer 2026-08-30 13:55:01 +0800 1239) 		\begin{cases}
20e0ae0f (Dengnifer 2026-08-30 13:55:01 +0800 1240) 			1/(2m-k) & \text{if } \{u,v\} \in E\,,\\
20e0ae0f (Dengnifer 2026-08-30 13:55:01 +0800 1241) 			0 & \text{otherwise}\,.
20e0ae0f (Dengnifer 2026-08-30 13:55:01 +0800 1242) 		\end{cases}
20e0ae0f (Dengnifer 2026-08-30 13:55:01 +0800 1243) 	\]
20e0ae0f (Dengnifer 2026-08-30 13:55:01 +0800 1244) 	This is identical to the uniform distribution over ordered pairs $(u,v) \in U \times U$ such that $\{u,v\} \in E$: each edge that is not a self-loop contributes two ordered pairs, and each self-loop contributes one, whence the normalization $2m-k$.
20e0ae0f (Dengnifer 2026-08-30 13:55:01 +0800 1245) \end{definition}
20e0ae0f (Dengnifer 2026-08-30 13:55:01 +0800 1246) 
bf889b51 (Dengnifer 2026-09-04 17:57:46 +0800 1247) \begin{lemma}[Formalization support for normalization of the graph distribution]\label{lem:graph-distribution-mass}
4c7e7295 (Dengnifer 2026-09-04 17:19:42 +0800 1248) 	\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.graphDistribution_isProbability}
4c7e7295 (Dengnifer 2026-09-04 17:19:42 +0800 1249) 	\leanok
4c7e7295 (Dengnifer 2026-09-04 17:19:42 +0800 1250) 	\uses{def:graph-distribution}
bf889b51 (Dengnifer 2026-09-04 17:57:46 +0800 1251) 	This is not a named statement of the source article; it records the normalization of the graph distribution of Definition~\ref{def:graph-distribution}, which the source leaves implicit. Let $G = (U, E)$ be an undirected graph with nonempty edge set $E$. Then the graph distribution $\mu_G$ has total mass one, that is, $\sum_{(u,v) \in U \times U} \mu_G(u,v) = 1$.
4c7e7295 (Dengnifer 2026-09-04 17:19:42 +0800 1252) \end{lemma}
4c7e7295 (Dengnifer 2026-09-04 17:19:42 +0800 1253) \begin{proof}
4c7e7295 (Dengnifer 2026-09-04 17:19:42 +0800 1254) 	\leanok
4c7e7295 (Dengnifer 2026-09-04 17:19:42 +0800 1255) 	\uses{def:graph-distribution}
4c7e7295 (Dengnifer 2026-09-04 17:19:42 +0800 1256) 	The distribution $\mu_G$ is uniform on the set of ordered pairs $(u,v) \in U \times U$ with $\{u,v\} \in E$, and that set is nonempty because $E$ is: an edge $\{u,v\} \in E$ yields the ordered pair $(u,v)$. A uniform distribution on a nonempty finite set has total mass one.
4c7e7295 (Dengnifer 2026-09-04 17:19:42 +0800 1257) \end{proof}

